<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guy I de la Roche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1263">1263</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Guy I, Duke of Athens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guy I de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a> was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Thebes from 1225 to 1263, succeeding his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho-de-la-roche">Otho de la Roche</a>. He rebelled against William of Villehardouin, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, and was defeated at Mount Karydi in 1258, but was pardoned after appearing before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, who allowed him to style himself Duke of Athens. His long reign was a time of prosperity for the Frankish duchy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Guy held the whole of Thebes, for which, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, he owed homage to the prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> itself answered to no sovereign but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperor after the fall of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1224. The duchy prospered from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry, centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, and from its trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. In 1240 Guy gave half of the lordship of Thebes to Bela of St. Omer, the husband of his sister Bonne. In 1251 he bought the lordship of Argos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> from his brother Othon, lord of Ray, for 15,000 hyperpyra and his own lands and claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>When Prince William II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> disputed the suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> with the Venetians and the local triarchs, Guy took their side. In the spring of 1258 William defeated him at the foot of Mount Karydi, besieged him in Thebes and forced him to surrender. Guy did homage at Nikli, but the barons of Achaea, not being his peers, sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for judgement, and he left in the spring of 1259. The court of France found that he did not owe liege homage and so could not be deprived of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>; the journey was to be his punishment.</p><p>Setting out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in the spring of 1260, Guy learned that Emperor Michael VIII Palaeologus had defeated and captured William II at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a>, and soon after his return came news that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> had fallen to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. Guy administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> while William was held prisoner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Guy survived these upheavals of the Frankish states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> until his death in 1263 and was succeeded by his son John I, who died unmarried and childless in 1280. His wife, whose name does not survive, was a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a> from Champagne or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Their son William followed John as duke and, by Helena Angelina Komnene, was the father of Guy II. Of their daughters, Alice, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, married John II of Beirut, and Marguerite married Count Henry I of Vaudémont.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guy I de la Roche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under William II Achaea was the leading military power of Latin Greece, and in the War of the Euboeote Succession he defeated <b>Guy I de la Roche</b>, lord of Athens, at the Battle of Karydi in 1258.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His nephew <b>Guy I</b> was styled Duke of Athens by the mid-13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-athens">Duchy of Athens</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doubs">Doubs</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.17,6.42&amp;year=1253" aria-label="Doubs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Guy I de la Roche"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5372 6070h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5372 6070h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.17,6.42&amp;year=1253">What was here in 1253? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
